title: SharePoint ToolShell
page: breaches/sharepoint-toolshell.html
as-of: 2026-11-23
promise: Mass exploitation of on-premises SharePoint came to light around July 18, 2025: attackers stole servers' ASP.NET machine keys, and requests forged with them survive patching until the keys are rotated.

== At a glance
layout: stats
stat: None | credentials needed | unauthenticated remote code execution
stat: 400+ | compromised servers found | in Eye Security's own scanning, reported by its July 29 update
stat: 75-85+ | compromised servers, estimated | in Bitsight's July 22, 2025 brief
Stolen machine keys outlive the patch, and the attacker stays until they are rotated. SharePoint Online in Microsoft 365 was not affected.
notes:
Start with what the page leads with.

No credential was needed. A crafted request to on-premises SharePoint Server gave remote code execution, RCE: running code of the attacker's choosing on someone else's server.

The two counts come from different sources. Eye Security reported finding more than 400 compromised servers in its own scanning, by its July 29 update. Bitsight's July 22, 2025 brief put compromises at an estimated 75 to 85 or more servers globally. Keep each figure with its source, and keep the word estimated on Bitsight's.

The point the page stresses has no number. Stolen machine keys outlive the patch: an attacker holding them can return through requests indistinguishable from normal traffic, and stays until the keys are rotated. SharePoint Online in Microsoft 365 was not affected.

Among those hit, the page names the US Department of Energy, which confirmed that exploitation affected a very small number of its systems, including at the National Nuclear Security Administration.

== What happened, and when
layout: timeline
event: As early as July 7, 2025 | Attempts on two SharePoint flaws, the original pair, Microsoft's analysis suggests
event: July 17 to 19 | Waves Eye Security saw; it identified the mass exploitation on July 18
event: July 22 | Bitsight estimates 75 to 85 or more compromised servers
event: By July 29 | Eye Security reports finding 400+ compromised servers
Microsoft then published CVE-2025-53770 and CVE-2025-53771 for related flaws; it marks only the first as exploited. The page gives no date. Eye Security later said the waves seem to have used the original pair. Spacing is not to scale.
notes:
The timeline shows dates the page gives, in order, and no others.

Microsoft's analysis suggests attackers were attempting to exploit two SharePoint flaws, CVE-2025-49704 and CVE-2025-49706, as early as July 7, 2025. Keep the words analysis suggests and attempting. A CVE is a public identifier for a disclosed vulnerability.

Mass exploitation came to light around July 18. Eye Security identified it on July 18 and saw waves of it from July 17 to 19. Microsoft then published CVE-2025-53770 and CVE-2025-53771 for related flaws, with updates that include more robust protections than the original fixes. Of those two, it marks only CVE-2025-53770 as exploited. Eye Security later corrected its account: the waves it saw seem to have used the original pair, not the new CVEs.

Bitsight's July 22 brief estimated 75 to 85 or more compromised servers. By its July 29 update, Eye Security reported finding more than 400 in its own scanning.

Microsoft attributed some activity to Storm-2603, which went on to deploy Warlock ransomware. The page gives no date for that or for Microsoft's new CVEs. The markers are evenly spaced, so read them as order only.

== Who says what, and how sure
layout: table
row: Claim | Who | How sure
row: Some activity was Storm-2603 | Microsoft | Some, not all
row: Storm-2603 is China-based | Microsoft | Moderate confidence
row: Storm-2603 deployed Warlock through this access | Microsoft | Observed
row: Exploit attempts as early as July 7 | Microsoft | Its analysis suggests
row: July 17 to 19 waves used the original pair | Eye Security, correcting itself | Seem to have
The page asks that Microsoft's confidence language be preserved.
notes:
The page is careful about who says what, and this slide keeps that.

Microsoft attributed some of the exploitation to Storm-2603, and tracks part of the activity under that name. It assesses Storm-2603 with moderate confidence as China-based. The page says that confidence language is doing real work and should be preserved rather than flattened into a claim. Microsoft also observed Warlock ransomware deployed through this access, and that cluster went on to deploy it. Multiple actors moved on these CVEs, so Storm-2603 is part of the activity, not all of it.

Microsoft's analysis suggests attempts on CVE-2025-49704 and CVE-2025-49706 as early as July 7. Eye Security, correcting its own account, says the waves it saw from July 17 to 19 seem to have used that original pair, not the new CVEs. Keep suggests and seem to have.

== 400+ found by Eye Security, 75-85+ estimated by Bitsight
layout: compare
col: 400+ found | Eye Security | More than 400 compromised servers, in its own scanning | Reported by its July 29 update
col: 75-85+ estimated | Bitsight | An estimate of compromised servers globally | In its July 22, 2025 brief
The page gives both figures, each with its source and date.
notes:
The page gives two figures for how many servers were compromised, each with its source.

Bitsight's July 22, 2025 brief put compromises at an estimated 75 to 85 or more servers globally. By its July 29 update, Eye Security reported finding more than 400 compromised servers in its own scanning.

The page words them differently: Bitsight's is an estimate, and Eye Security's is what its own scanning found. When you use either number, say whose it is and when it was given, and keep the word estimate on Bitsight's.

== The kill chain on one slide
layout: flow
step: 01 | Initial Access | One POST to a settings page | T1190
step: 02 | Persistence | A web shell in layouts | T1505.003
step: 03 | Credential Access | Machine keys extracted | T1552.004
step: 04 | Stealth | Forged __VIEWSTATE | T1606
step: 05 | Lateral Movement | Sits next to everything | T1078.002
step: 06 | Impact | Warlock ransomware | T1486
Arrows follow the page's stage order. Stages 05 and 06 draw on what Microsoft observed of Storm-2603, one of multiple actors.
notes:
Here is the whole chain on one slide: the page's six stages, each with its phase and primary ATT&CK technique.

Stage 01 is initial access: one crafted HTTP POST to ToolPane.aspx, a web part settings page, gave unauthenticated remote code execution. Stage 02 is persistence, a web shell in SharePoint's layouts folder. Stage 03 is credential access: the attackers extracted the ASP.NET machine keys. Stage 04, which the page files under Stealth, is the forged __VIEWSTATE requests those keys allow. Stage 05, under Lateral Movement, covers where on-premises SharePoint sits and what Microsoft saw Storm-2603 do next: steal credentials, move laterally and change Group Policy to distribute Warlock. Stage 06 is impact: Microsoft attributed some of the exploitation to Storm-2603, which went on to deploy Warlock ransomware.

Read the arrows as the page's stage order. The next slides take each stage in turn.

== One POST to ToolPane.aspx ran code, no credential needed
layout: stage
stage: 01
phase: Initial Access
technique: T1190 | Exploit Public-Facing Application
fact: Endpoint | ToolPane.aspx, normally used to manage web part settings
fact: Bug class | deserialization of untrusted data (CVE-2025-53770)
fact: Reached through | an authentication bypass, CVE-2025-53771
fact: Exposure | on-premises SharePoint is very often published to the internet
No credential and no user interaction: reachability was the entire prerequisite.
notes:
Stage 01 is how the attackers got in. The page maps it to T1190, Exploit Public-Facing Application. Its CVEs are CVE-2025-53770 and CVE-2025-53771, and the original pair they relate to, CVE-2025-49704 and CVE-2025-49706. The deserialization flaw is CVE-2025-53770, reached through an authentication bypass, CVE-2025-53771, which Microsoft classes as spoofing. An authentication bypass lets a request skip the sign-in check.

A crafted HTTP POST to ToolPane.aspx, an ordinary endpoint for managing web part settings, triggered deserialization of untrusted input and unauthenticated remote code execution. An HTTP POST is a request that sends data to the server, and a web part is a configurable building block on a SharePoint page.

The page calls unsafe deserialization a decades-old bug class and still one of the most reliable paths to code execution in enterprise .NET applications, because deserializing attacker-controlled data means instantiating attacker-chosen types and running their constructors.

No authentication was required, so the prerequisite was simply network reachability. On-premises SharePoint is very often published to the internet precisely so that remote staff and partners can use it. SharePoint Online was not affected.

The next slide explains deserialization in general terms.

== What deserializing untrusted data can do
layout: background
source: OWASP Deserialization Cheat Sheet | https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Deserialization_Cheat_Sheet.html
Serialization turns an object into data; deserialization rebuilds the object from it. OWASP says attacks on deserializers have allowed denial of service, access control attacks or remote code execution.

In .NET, a type check after deserialization comes too late.
notes:
Background, from OWASP's Deserialization Cheat Sheet, in general terms.

Serialization is the process of turning an object into a data format that can be restored later. Deserialization is the reverse: taking data structured in some format and rebuilding it into an object. Today JSON is the most popular format, and before it XML; many languages also have native serialization formats with more features.

OWASP says those features can sometimes be repurposed for malicious effect when they operate on untrusted data, and that attacks against deserializers have been found to allow denial of service, access control attacks or remote code execution.

For .NET, OWASP notes that a deserializer can only instantiate types it knows about, and lists known .NET types that can be abused for remote code execution. It warns that checking an object's type after deserialization is too late, because execution may already have occurred.

== A web shell in the layouts folder made one request a foothold
layout: stage
stage: 02
phase: Persistence
technique: T1505.003 | Server Software Component: Web Shell
fact: Artifact | web shells including spinstall0.aspx
fact: Location | the layouts folder, full of legitimate .aspx files
fact: Capability | persistent command execution and staging of further malware
fact: The trap | deleting it feels like remediation, and is not
The attacker has usually already taken something shell removal will not affect.
notes:
Stage 02 is persistence. The page maps it to T1505.003, Server Software Component: Web Shell.

Attackers planted web shells such as spinstall0.aspx in SharePoint's layouts directory, giving persistent command execution and a stage for further payloads. An .aspx file is an ASP.NET web page. The layouts folder legitimately contains many of them, so one more does not stand out to a human browsing the directory. The observed shells fetched and ran further malware from attacker infrastructure, converting a single exploited request into an interactive foothold on the server.

This is the artifact most defenders went looking for once the advisories landed. The page warns that finding and deleting it feels like resolution, and that this is exactly the trap the next step sets: by the time the shell is found, the attacker has usually already taken something that removing it will not affect.

The next slide explains web shells in general terms.

== What ATT&CK calls a web shell
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1505.003 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1505/003/
A web shell is a web script placed on an openly accessible web server, letting an adversary use the server as a gateway into a network. It may provide functions to execute, or a command line on the host.
notes:
Background, from MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

ATT&CK files web shells under Server Software Component, a Persistence technique. Adversaries may backdoor web servers with web shells to establish persistent access to systems.

A web shell is a web script that is placed on an openly accessible web server, to allow an adversary to access the web server as a gateway into a network. It may provide a set of functions to execute, or a command-line interface on the system that hosts the web server.

Besides the server-side script, ATT&CK says, a web shell may have a client interface program that is used to talk to the web server.

The page maps its stage 02 to this technique.

== The attackers extracted the server's ASP.NET machine keys
layout: stage
stage: 03
phase: Credential Access
technique: T1552.004 | Unsecured Credentials: Private Keys
fact: Stolen | ASP.NET ValidationKey and DecryptionKey
fact: Purpose | signing and encrypting __VIEWSTATE, trusted client-side state
fact: Effect of theft | the ability to produce state the application accepts as its own
fact: Why forgotten | rarely rotated, inventoried or thought of as credentials
The page calls the keys the server's identity to itself.
notes:
Stage 03 is the move the page says elevates ToolShell above a typical web application compromise. The page maps it to T1552.004, Unsecured Credentials: Private Keys.

The attackers extracted the ASP.NET machine keys, the ValidationKey and DecryptionKey, from the compromised server. ASP.NET uses them to sign and encrypt __VIEWSTATE, the mechanism by which the framework trusts state that round-trips through the client, the user's browser. Whoever holds those keys can produce state the application will accept as its own.

The page calls them the server's identity to itself. Unlike a password, nobody thinks of them as a credential with a rotation policy. Most administrators have never touched them, and many do not know they exist.

The page reads this alongside Storm-0558, which has its own kill chain on csoh.org, and calls the shared lesson stark: theft of signing material converts a bounded intrusion into indefinite trusted access. That comes back after the stages.

The next slide explains view state and machine keys in general terms.

== What ASP.NET view state and machine keys are
layout: background
source: Microsoft Learn, ASP.NET View State Overview | https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/aspnet/bb386448(v=vs.100)
credit: Also from machineKey Element (ASP.NET Settings Schema) (learn.microsoft.com)
View state carries a page's values between round trips in a hidden field. ASP.NET adds a keyed hash, rechecks it on postback, and raises an exception on a mismatch. The machineKey element configures the keys.
notes:
Background, from Microsoft Learn's ASP.NET documentation, in general terms.

A web application is stateless: a new instance of the page class is created for every request. View state is how the ASP.NET page framework preserves page and control values between round trips. When the page is rendered, those values are serialized into base64-encoded strings and put in a hidden field or fields in the page.

Microsoft's security note says it is easy for a malicious user to see and modify the contents of a hidden field. So by default ASP.NET creates a hash of the view state data with a key and adds it to the encoded data. When the page is posted back, the framework recomputes the hash and compares the two; if they do not match, it raises an exception indicating the view state might be invalid. View state can also be encrypted.

The machineKey element configures those keys. The validationKey is used to validate data, and the decryptionKey to encrypt and decrypt forms-authentication data, and view state when view state is encrypted.

Also from machineKey Element (ASP.NET Settings Schema): https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/dotnet/netframework-4.0/w8h3skw9(v=vs.100)

== Forged __VIEWSTATE signed with the stolen keys passed as legitimate
layout: stage
stage: 04
phase: Stealth
technique: T1606 | Forge Web Credentials
technique: T1550 | Use Alternate Authentication Material
fact: Technique | forged __VIEWSTATE payloads signed with the stolen keys
fact: Application's view | a correctly signed, entirely legitimate request
fact: Result | authenticated command execution that survives patching
fact: Recovery needs | rotating the machine keys, then restarting IIS
Until the keys are rotated, log review will not separate this from legitimate use.
notes:
Stage 04 is what the stolen keys buy. The page files it under Stealth and maps it to T1606, Forge Web Credentials, and T1550, Use Alternate Authentication Material.

With the keys, attackers forged __VIEWSTATE payloads that SharePoint accepted as legitimate, giving authenticated command execution that survives patching. To the application, a forged __VIEWSTATE signed with the real keys is a correctly signed request, and SharePoint validates it exactly as it validates its own. The page calls this the most important operational fact about ToolShell, and the one most likely to be missed during response.

Multiple analyses described the result as a permanent backdoor. The page calls the phrasing fair, with a condition: until the machine keys are rotated, the attacker can return through traffic that is cryptographically indistinguishable from legitimate use, and no amount of log review will separate the two.

Recovery requires rotating the machine keys, then restarting IIS. IIS, Internet Information Services, is Microsoft's web server.

== What patching, shell removal and key rotation each do
layout: compare
col: Patching | Closes the entry path | Does nothing about the copied key
col: Removing the web shell | Deletes the artifact | Leaves the capability
col: Rotating keys, then restarting IIS | Required for recovery | Restarting IIS makes the new keys take effect
The page says everything else in a response is undone if key rotation is skipped.
notes:
The page lines up three response steps against what each one does.

Applying the patch closes the door the attacker came through, and does nothing about the key they copied on the way in. Removing the web shell deletes the artifact and leaves the capability.

Recovery needs the third step: rotate the machine keys, then restart IIS so the new keys take effect. The page calls this the most important instruction on the page, and says everything else in a response is undone if it is skipped, because forged __VIEWSTATE traffic is cryptographically valid and will not be found in logs.

Stage 06 comes back to what can happen when an organization takes the first two steps and not the third.

== On-premises SharePoint is domain-joined, rich in service accounts and full of documents
layout: stage
stage: 05
phase: Lateral Movement
technique: T1078.002 | Valid Accounts: Domain Accounts
technique: T1213.002 | Data from Information Repositories: Sharepoint
fact: Position | domain-joined; service accounts often hold excess privilege
fact: Content | network documentation, pasted credentials, contracts, HR files
fact: Storm-2603, per Microsoft | Mimikatz against LSASS, then lateral movement with PsExec and Impacket
Those hit tend to have kept SharePoint on-premises for regulatory or data residency reasons.
notes:
Stage 05 covers where the server sits, and what Microsoft saw happen next. The page files it under Lateral Movement and maps it to T1078.002, Valid Accounts: Domain Accounts, and T1213.002, Data from Information Repositories: Sharepoint.

On-premises SharePoint is rarely isolated, the page says. It is domain-joined, and its service accounts frequently hold more Active Directory privilege than anyone intended. It stores the corpus an intruder most wants next: network documentation, credentials pasted into pages, contracts, HR files and project material.

Microsoft described what Storm-2603 did after getting in through SharePoint. It used Mimikatz against LSASS memory to extract plaintext credentials, moved laterally with PsExec and the Impacket toolkit, running commands over Windows Management Instrumentation, and then modified Group Policy Objects to distribute Warlock ransomware. LSASS is the Windows process that handles sign-in and holds credentials in memory, and Group Policy Objects push settings to machines across a domain.

The organizations hit tend to be the ones that kept SharePoint on-premises for regulatory or data-residency reasons, which correlates with holding exactly the material worth stealing.

== Storm-2603, per Microsoft, was behind some exploitation and deployed Warlock ransomware
layout: stage
stage: 06
phase: Impact
technique: T1486 | Data Encrypted for Impact
fact: Attributed cluster | Storm-2603, China-based per Microsoft, with moderate confidence
fact: Payload | Warlock ransomware
fact: Multiple actors | typical once a reliable unauthenticated exploit is public
fact: Dangerous sequence | patch, delete the shell, skip key rotation
Without rotation, an organization could be encrypted weeks later, with no idea how.
notes:
Stage 06 is the impact. The page maps it to T1486, Data Encrypted for Impact. Ransomware is malware that encrypts a victim's data and demands payment to restore it.

Multiple actors moved on these CVEs, which the page calls the normal pattern once a reliable unauthenticated exploit for widely deployed software becomes known. Microsoft tracks part of the activity as Storm-2603, assessed with moderate confidence to be China-based. The page asks you to note that confidence language, which is doing real work, and to preserve it rather than flatten it into a claim. That cluster went on to deploy Warlock ransomware, which Microsoft observed deployed through this access.

The page asks you to sit with the combination: an unauthenticated internet-facing remote code execution, a persistence mechanism that survives patching, and a ransomware payload at the end. An organization that patched promptly, removed the web shell and did not rotate its machine keys could be encrypted weeks later, and have no idea how the actor got back in.

== Stolen machine keys outlive the patch
layout: text
The page reads ToolShell alongside Storm-0558: theft of signing material converts a bounded intrusion into indefinite trusted access.
- Machine keys are rarely rotated, inventoried or thought of as credentials.
- Everything downstream is designed to believe them.
notes:
This is the pattern the page draws from the chain.

At stage 03 the page reads ToolShell alongside Storm-0558, and calls the shared lesson stark: theft of signing material converts a bounded intrusion into indefinite trusted access. It is the failure that is hardest to recover from, the page says, because everything downstream is designed to believe it.

Machine keys show why. The page says they are rarely rotated, rarely inventoried and rarely thought of as credentials, and that almost nobody tracks them, which is why stealing them works so well.

In its defenses, the page says the Storm-0558 chain makes the same argument from the cloud side. Storm-0558 has its own kill chain on csoh.org, linked at the end of this deck.

== Six defenses against six stages
layout: matrix
columns: 01 | 02 | 03 | 04 | 05 | 06
row: Rotate machine keys, then restart IIS | 03 | 04 | 06
row: Off the public internet | 01
row: Hunt from the start of the window | 01 | 02
row: Limit egress and the service account | 02 | 05
row: Inventory signing material | 03
row: Emergency patch path in hours | 01
Key rotation reaches stage 06 through the page's own scenario of skipped rotation. The patch path reaches stage 01, whose entry path the page says patching closes.
notes:
This matrix sets the page's six defenses against the six stages. A cell is marked only where the page's own words tie that defense to that stage.

Rotating the machine keys answers stage 03, since the page says to assume the ValidationKey and DecryptionKey are stolen, and stage 04, where the page names it as required for recovery from forged __VIEWSTATE. It reaches stage 06 through the page's own scenario: an organization that patched and removed the web shell but did not rotate its keys could be encrypted weeks later.

Getting servers off the public internet answers stage 01, where reachability was the entire prerequisite. Hunting from the start of the window answers stage 01 through IIS logs of POST requests to ToolPane.aspx, and stage 02 through unexpected .aspx files such as spinstall0.aspx. Default-deny egress breaks stage 02's payload fetching, in the page's words, and auditing the service account answers stage 05. Inventorying signing material answers stage 03's forgotten keys.

The emergency patch path answers stage 01: the page says applying the patch closes the door the attacker came through. Its own words are about speed: active exploitation to public advisory to broad opportunistic scanning within days.

== Rotate the keys, and inventory signing material
layout: compare
col: Rotate machine keys (03, 04, 06) | If the server was exposed in the exploitation window, assume the keys stolen | Rotate them, then restart IIS so the new keys take effect | Patching alone leaves the attacker inside
col: Inventory signing material (03) | ASP.NET machine keys, SAML signing certificates, JWT signing keys, API signing secrets | Each with an owner, a rotation interval and a tested rotation procedure
The page calls key rotation its most important instruction.
notes:
Two defenses answer the stolen keys.

First, rotate the machine keys, then restart IIS. The page calls this the most important instruction on the page. If a SharePoint server was exposed during the exploitation window, assume the ValidationKey and DecryptionKey are stolen, rotate them, and restart IIS so the new keys take effect. Patching alone leaves the attacker inside, and everything else in the response is undone if this step is skipped, because forged __VIEWSTATE traffic is cryptographically valid and will not be found in logs.

Second, inventory cryptographic material as credentials with owners and rotation schedules. Almost nobody tracks machine keys, which is why stealing them works so well. Build an inventory of signing and encryption material across the estate: ASP.NET machine keys, SAML signing certificates, JWT signing keys and API signing secrets, each with an owner, a rotation interval and a tested rotation procedure. SAML, the Security Assertion Markup Language, and JWT, the JSON Web Token, are formats for signed identity data.

== Take SharePoint off the internet, and limit what it can reach
layout: compare
col: Off the public internet (01) | Reachability was the entire prerequisite | Put SharePoint behind a VPN or identity-aware proxy | Or move to SharePoint Online, which this vulnerability did not affect
col: Egress, service account (02, 05) | Default-deny egress from the host breaks payload fetching | Audit the service account's Active Directory privilege | Apply least privilege; tier administrative accounts
Surplus service account privilege turns a web compromise into a domain problem, the page says.
notes:
Two defenses limit who can reach the server, and what the server can reach.

First, get on-premises collaboration servers off the public internet. The vulnerability was unauthenticated, so reachability was the entire prerequisite. Put SharePoint behind a VPN or an identity-aware proxy, or move to SharePoint Online, which this vulnerability did not affect. An identity-aware proxy checks who is asking before a request reaches the application. The page reads the Microsoft 365 exclusion as a statement about where the maintenance burden lands: someone has to patch it within hours, and it does not have to be you.

Second, constrain what the server can do outbound and what its service account can reach. Web shells fetched further payloads from attacker infrastructure, so default-deny egress from the SharePoint host breaks that stage. Egress is outbound traffic, and default-deny blocks every destination not explicitly allowed. Then audit the service account: the page says SharePoint service accounts routinely accumulate Active Directory privilege far past what the application needs. Apply least privilege and tier your administrative accounts.

== Hunt from the start of the exploitation window
layout: table
row: Where | What to look for
row: Layouts folder | Unexpected .aspx files, including spinstall0.aspx
row: IIS logs | POST requests to ToolPane.aspx
row: SharePoint worker process | Unusual child processes it spawns
Absence of a web shell is not absence of compromise: the keys may already be gone, and forged __VIEWSTATE traffic will not show in logs.
notes:
Assume compromise from the start of the exploitation window, and hunt accordingly. The page says exploitation was underway before advisories landed.

It names three places to look. Search the layouts folder for unexpected .aspx files, including spinstall0.aspx. Review IIS logs for POST requests to ToolPane.aspx. And look for unusual child processes spawned by the SharePoint worker process, the process that runs the web application. A child process is a program another process starts.

Keep the page's caveat with these. Absence of a web shell is not absence of compromise, because the keys may already be gone. And the page says forged __VIEWSTATE traffic is cryptographically valid and will not be found in logs, so log review will not separate it from legitimate use.

== An emergency patch path measured in hours
layout: text
ToolShell went from active exploitation to public advisory to broad opportunistic scanning within days. The page says any internet-facing enterprise application needs a pre-authorized change path, a named approver and a rehearsed rollback.
notes:
The sixth defense is about speed. The page says patching closes stage 01's entry path, and wants that done within hours.

ToolShell went from active exploitation to public advisory to broad opportunistic scanning within days. The page says any internet-facing enterprise application needs a pre-authorized change path, a named approver and a rehearsed rollback, so that the decision to patch immediately is not being made for the first time during an active campaign. A rollback is the way back to the previous version when a change breaks something.

The page points to its vulnerability management page for more. It is linked at the end of this deck.

Keep stage 04's limit in mind alongside this one: patching closes the entry path, and does nothing about a key already copied.

== Check your own environment
layout: checklist
check: Was a server exposed in the window? Are its keys rotated and IIS restarted?
check: Is on-premises SharePoint reachable from the internet without a VPN or identity-aware proxy?
check: Have you searched the layouts folder and IIS logs from the window's start?
check: Can the SharePoint host reach any destination outbound?
check: How much Active Directory privilege do your SharePoint service accounts hold?
check: Does every signing key have an owner, a rotation interval and a tested procedure?
check: Could you patch an internet-facing system within hours, with a named approver?
notes:
Turn the page's defenses into questions about your own estate. Each question comes from one of them.

Ask whether any SharePoint server was exposed during the exploitation window, and if so, whether its machine keys have been rotated and IIS restarted. Ask whether on-premises SharePoint is reachable from the internet without a VPN or identity-aware proxy.

Ask whether anyone has searched the layouts folder for unexpected .aspx files, IIS logs for POST requests to ToolPane.aspx, and the worker process for unusual child processes, from the start of the window. Ask whether the SharePoint host can reach any destination outbound, and how much Active Directory privilege its service accounts hold.

Finally, ask whether every signing key, from ASP.NET machine keys to SAML signing certificates and JWT signing keys, has an owner, a rotation interval and a tested rotation procedure, and whether you could patch an internet-facing system within hours.

== You patched and deleted the web shell. Are you done?
layout: question
Your SharePoint server was exposed during the exploitation window. You applied the patch, found spinstall0.aspx in the layouts folder and deleted it, and your logs look clean.

What is still open, and how would you know?
notes:
Give the room a minute with this one before moving on.

The scenario is the one the page warns about. Patching closes the entry path, and removing the web shell deletes the artifact. The page says finding and deleting the shell feels like resolution, and calls that a trap.

Ask each person what is still open, and how they would tell, before you show the answer slide. Watch for answers that lean on the clean logs.

== The keys may already be gone
layout: answer
Assume the ValidationKey and DecryptionKey are stolen. Rotate them, then restart IIS so the new keys take effect.
- Forged __VIEWSTATE traffic is cryptographically valid, and will not show in logs.
- Absence of a web shell is not absence of compromise.
notes:
The page's answer is the machine keys.

If a SharePoint server was exposed during the exploitation window, assume the ValidationKey and DecryptionKey are stolen, rotate them, and restart IIS so the new keys take effect. The page calls this its most important instruction.

Clean logs settle nothing here. Forged __VIEWSTATE traffic is cryptographically valid, the page says you will not find it in logs, and no amount of log review will separate it from legitimate use. Absence of a web shell is not absence of compromise, because the keys may already be gone.

Stage 06 gives the stakes: an organization that patched promptly, removed the web shell and did not rotate its machine keys could be encrypted weeks later, and have no idea how the actor got back in.

== Which of your signing keys has no owner?
layout: question
The page says almost nobody tracks machine keys, which is why stealing them works so well.

Name one signing or encryption key in your estate with no owner, no rotation interval or no tested rotation procedure.
notes:
This question comes from the page's inventory defense and from the pattern it draws with Storm-0558.

The page says machine keys are rarely rotated, rarely inventoried and rarely thought of as credentials, and that theft of signing material converts a bounded intrusion into indefinite trusted access.

Ask each person to name one specific key from their own environment before you show the answer slide. Machine keys are one example; the page's list goes wider.

== Inventory signing material as credentials
layout: answer
The page asks for an inventory of signing and encryption material, each with an owner, a rotation interval and a tested rotation procedure.
- ASP.NET machine keys and SAML signing certificates
- JWT signing keys and API signing secrets
notes:
The page's answer is to treat cryptographic material as credentials, with owners and rotation schedules.

Build an inventory of signing and encryption material across the estate: ASP.NET machine keys, SAML signing certificates, JWT signing keys and API signing secrets. Give each one an owner, a rotation interval and a tested rotation procedure.

The page says the Storm-0558 chain makes the same argument from the cloud side.

Bring the group's examples back to the three things the page says each key needs: an owner, a rotation interval and a tested rotation procedure.

